#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :question/id #uuid "f34de8cd-b650-5926-9f06-8127d4fb3a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-2393" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049922 :topic/math-academy-id 2536 :topic/title "Homogeneous Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b387a19d-7cf7-5c7c-b4a0-3c531f2dc207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2}y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087276} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fc4204-9ff1-5434-b8c8-b243a9b5a4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2xy}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087277} {:answer/id #uuid "e8891a45-78f7-5a8b-a02d-8cc9d4f8af08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087278} {:answer/id #uuid "37572047-280a-508a-ac11-6a5fe1712a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087279} {:answer/id #uuid "7253b068-0bec-5847-aaa9-da6e7449baf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy}{x + y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087280}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fc06f993-8bb8-5bba-9060-505b57e636e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087275} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6836dad2-0a5e-566e-82e6-ae2f1e00252c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395952} {:answer/id #uuid "02b3bcfe-b179-54e6-9f39-7cf2b8a2012c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395953} {:answer/id #uuid "501f1379-0915-537f-a01b-ac50f840b151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395954} {:answer/id #uuid "f65a218e-09b9-5f93-833d-5222a9ab1b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395955} {:answer/id #uuid "9438294d-1900-5c07-88ff-915745bfe633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65a218e-09b9-5f93-833d-5222a9ab1b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395955} :answer-field/id #uuid "f23bfdfe-607b-5b9b-8499-14f49fb7f966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395951}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32964a8b-4376-5c16-9f30-00e530a869bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-8739" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{2(tx)(ty)}{(tx)^{2}+(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ee7f59-7955-5e6d-97a3-31d520538549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352078} {:answer/id #uuid "a4501cde-34bb-5948-b58a-a742171c4d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352970} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c2162-7781-5446-ba96-31792294d28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353181} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a895b9-f9ad-59f2-9ce7-b2f5eae6b151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353426} {:answer/id #uuid "f282b685-5577-5d72-ac2d-ce3ef3882ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c2162-7781-5446-ba96-31792294d28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353181} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08fe0a1-984f-586e-afda-76db1f02247e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354234}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c38fa75-d73d-55f8-bbc1-1dea4b79194b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8741" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=x.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $1.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =tx=tf(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06801954-aceb-527f-87ea-95cb0f6de8cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}x-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272446} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca68fa9-62e4-5b19-b251-d7820af169fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272908} {:answer/id #uuid "610b5f06-7106-5295-bbce-136ddf73ac09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3xy+xy}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273225} {:answer/id #uuid "897cd727-6340-5a7f-a411-d1eec1449527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273572} {:answer/id #uuid "94291e38-c665-530b-aa62-ffcb098f902f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca68fa9-62e4-5b19-b251-d7820af169fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272908} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eb6e88a-8ce7-5065-891e-2b9f914a957e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc348cb6-a91b-542b-84f6-f5b771ae4157" :question/math-academy-id "q-14319" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $3.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $3-3=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{3}-3(tx)^{2}(ty)+(tx)(ty)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}+(tx)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3})-{t}^{3}(3{x}^{2}y)+{t}^{3}(x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4875ea-48e5-5361-a8c6-15e5b78c5e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272504} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee30a5d-c60d-51de-8718-366ce9c6d7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x+y}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272925} {:answer/id #uuid "6437d2a5-9b98-5547-b4b2-8979038ea84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273247} {:answer/id #uuid "77c9d0da-c801-5a3a-a296-9ff45087e3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273408} {:answer/id #uuid "89bcafdd-55e6-5e96-9617-87e8ef808b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6437d2a5-9b98-5547-b4b2-8979038ea84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273247} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a0fc63-6ea7-5103-a6b4-659ab0b835cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274926}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9372b90-e355-5e25-8427-4c0e772f932c" :question/math-academy-id "q-14322" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}.$\n\nWe see that the numerator is NOT a homogeneous polynomial. Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is NOT a homogeneous function.\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\nFor $x=1$, $y=2$ and $t=3$, for instance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =f(2\\cdot 1,2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =f(2,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{6}^{2}+2(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =f(1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}+1(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, there exists at least one combination of values of $x$, $y$ and $t$ for which the identity $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ is not observed.\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c733e-9f50-59f1-a6c2-d2c461d141a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355126} {:answer/id #uuid "567ab48d-395e-5349-9130-02d9670bd228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355436} {:answer/id #uuid "6402d895-e12f-57c4-ad2c-f9930e976cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355555} {:answer/id #uuid "6404e39a-62ed-5cc9-8470-5899b49795e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355556} {:answer/id #uuid "f94f8b4b-d656-5e4d-a481-417dad03fa18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6402d895-e12f-57c4-ad2c-f9930e976cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355555} :answer-field/id #uuid "1299ce9b-cffd-57b7-832d-bc7388915ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356951}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc83a0da-147c-536d-ae44-6851708bb02f" :question/math-academy-id "q-90597" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5069fb02-022e-544e-b7ca-c45166ca4bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404268} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcfa690-e3cd-5cc8-9177-2dc5f0ed3023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404269} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec33d3c-cfb8-5e66-a78e-a3afbcab3a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404270} {:answer/id #uuid "2b98ed15-9816-5e8c-8df1-651d5c3a7b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404271} {:answer/id #uuid "e5065de5-7957-55f6-bf19-b00b1fba3806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{y-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec33d3c-cfb8-5e66-a78e-a3afbcab3a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404270} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19317f0-552c-5bb1-aa73-1ed888b17d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404267}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29260781-8760-5b93-aff1-74357dc9fb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8369" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100992 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100687 :question/id #uuid "1b15db42-aba2-575c-a01e-b715450329fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4379" :question/problem "Find $A+C-(B+A),$ if\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C-(B+A) & =A+C-B-A \\\\  & =\\cancel{A}+C-B-\\cancel{A} \\\\  & =C-B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-3 & -1-(-1) \\\\ 1-2 & 2-0 \\\\ -6-(-4) & 1-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e62b0e-adda-5d35-aeb9-961057dce8f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049939 :topic/math-academy-id 137 :topic/title "Distributing the Negative Sign in a Linear Expression"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7de1032-850c-5ea9-a1ae-e083486ff706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098116} {:answer/id #uuid "ee11b057-083a-50d4-a16e-71b940dfbd15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098117} {:answer/id #uuid "ee16e495-0276-53ba-a14b-1b616dfd5ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098118} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f5f24e-1c43-5e7d-b296-3a57df79f5fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098119} {:answer/id #uuid "1629458d-cb60-534c-8cfa-eae2c7fb4823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098120}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfe9c16-ae55-5741-bcb1-0c2f8628bb30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098115}] :question/id #uuid "899c9618-f379-5a50-97a1-fd2442d407ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-13535" :question/problem "Find $Q + R - (Q + P)$, if\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d0d5b8b-4428-5073-8c81-ebd4d03f5e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098122} {:answer/id #uuid "04d70404-4b26-53f0-9c5c-5e44fe5b9647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098123} {:answer/id #uuid "39e8426c-e203-5fdf-a93f-111238c8159f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098124} {:answer/id #uuid "71f20d62-7872-5e37-97e9-a23e08aef59f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098125} {:answer/id #uuid "75cc67b5-2545-59dc-9edf-f9e64c9bb09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098126}] :answer-field/id #uuid "11b67b69-785c-5ae0-bd01-ea693dbbfc6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098121} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f88e9a5-8759-5e71-b262-00b079bef50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388343} {:answer/id #uuid "f2609425-f61f-5689-a1b2-ad1766ba4f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388344} {:answer/id #uuid "065fbb44-49f9-5677-ae45-0db8eae4e9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388345} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7d6d9c-e6a7-5790-92dd-e8b2bca71761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388346} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fb0aa4-9a2d-5c94-a1be-3f68d1da0be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d7d6d9c-e6a7-5790-92dd-e8b2bca71761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388346} :answer-field/id #uuid "aefbe9b4-2591-5f99-bd99-dce5ef9f6719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60ff0898-484c-5468-ab70-2a2b8951960b" :question/math-academy-id "q-13536" :question/problem "Find $(A+B)-C-(B-C)$, if\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)-C-(B-C) & =A+B-C-B+C \\\\  & =A+\\cancel{B}-\\cancel{C}-\\cancel{B}+\\cancel{C} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single matrix. So, the result is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233433 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2422983-d4c6-5d6a-a655-61aaf19ac8a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-13550" :question/problem "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,L=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048f24d1-178b-5552-bac6-6a3442ea9fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266184} {:answer/id #uuid "07274542-72df-51ae-9747-e72e289132fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266206} {:answer/id #uuid "0d154ee7-4761-51d0-9501-6a2171925a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266262} {:answer/id #uuid "5166f1bd-1a23-5981-a02f-80311fc50264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266871} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8998f2-311c-5243-b155-02fb3a72a11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a8998f2-311c-5243-b155-02fb3a72a11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267225} :answer-field/id #uuid "c35cca58-1d0a-5976-b167-1406a1720c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268755}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52150036-21b2-593f-96c3-821ddd81a9cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-13543" :question/problem "Find $M+O-(N+M)$, if\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,N=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,O=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M+O-(N+M) & =M+O-N-M \\\\  & =\\cancel{M}+O-N-\\cancel{M} \\\\  & =O-N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O-N & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8-1 & 6-4 \\\\ 5-2 & 3-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec88c80f-a8ed-502a-bf82-d3f72f783c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec88c80f-a8ed-502a-bf82-d3f72f783c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293854} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ada07d1-b262-5536-98f4-ad7591dc1467" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db546ef4-33b1-5d16-a9a1-4f701c9e3518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db546ef4-33b1-5d16-a9a1-4f701c9e3518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293783} :answer-field/id #uuid "49cf1090-dc76-5f9b-9ed9-62afb95195b2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de58a3a4-d500-5910-a328-0b296db3af22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de58a3a4-d500-5910-a328-0b296db3af22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293797} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0d2cb5-6e08-5ec8-898e-38988817e2eb" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa27f727-da39-5d99-a587-4ab6fcad711a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa27f727-da39-5d99-a587-4ab6fcad711a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293916} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe73ae99-752d-5979-8360-0edba6d46d65" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ce69f40-2704-5d26-9959-08c4817035a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-279581" :question/problem "Find $A+B-(C+B)$, if\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $A+B-(C+B)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-(C+B) & =A+B-C-B \\\\  & =A+\\cancel{B}-C-\\cancel{B} \\\\  & =A-C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-C & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-8 & -5-(-2) \\\\ 7-(-4) & 0-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ 11 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b68de3c-cb94-530f-b0dd-c01abe1f9020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333551} {:answer/id #uuid "87de7fb7-03f9-5478-8fbe-a7f74728952b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 & -7 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334191} {:answer/id #uuid "a4270518-0222-5ac9-9763-f005bf1361fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334429} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8229c4-0fa6-507d-92ed-e13af72729d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334761} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bb880b-fa90-5238-ad68-e045badea49d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb8229c4-0fa6-507d-92ed-e13af72729d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334761} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e678cc5-6058-5486-829b-8adc462aca74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335424}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1938e380-4886-59d0-baa4-d58100804a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-69623" :question/problem "Find $G+H-(H+F)$, if\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,G=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G+H-(H+F) & =G+H-H-F \\\\  & =G+\\cancel{H}-\\cancel{H}-F \\\\  & =G-F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-F & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-2 & 4-(-1) & -5-2 \\\\ 1-3 & -2-4 & 2-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51d28e19-c3ca-50e4-a6f4-60336111654b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 25 \\\\ -57 & -23\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333738} {:answer/id #uuid "63ffbc9d-739f-5fe5-ac0a-1011970f027f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333896} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5cd556-2d8d-5f84-8385-0d6bafc0bc0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334385} {:answer/id #uuid "a42f6c68-8de5-5fe4-82c0-ff575f2981a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -10 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334430} {:answer/id #uuid "cede7fbe-684a-5527-ae59-96c57e3600d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cede7fbe-684a-5527-ae59-96c57e3600d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334796} :answer-field/id #uuid "a852f62a-ae69-55a6-9d50-5d36100a30ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04eb824d-a063-5a72-b972-ff13e73919ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-69627" :question/problem "Find $C+V-(N+C)$, if\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 25 \\\\ -57 & -23\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,N=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C+V-(N+C) & =C+V-N-C \\\\  & =\\cancel{C}+V-N-\\cancel{C} \\\\  & =V-N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-N & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-1 & 3-(-7) \\\\ 6-2 & -4-(-6) \\\\ -1-3 & -8-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum or Difference of Three or More Matrices Using Distributivity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110458 :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/math-academy-id "e-3484" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50848432dd18a4a5dc64d4e5a5cfb8b3e14e9fed6231774dd83ead5552cd463.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702 :topic/math-academy-id 1318 :topic/title "Rationalizing Denominators"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928 :topic/math-academy-id 611 :topic/title "The Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratios"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029 :topic/math-academy-id 323 :topic/title "Fractions of Fractions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221a4245-8d36-54ef-a82d-e80ee76d86ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257060} {:answer/id #uuid "77312d37-9886-5569-95f9-c48a271a75f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257837} {:answer/id #uuid "8319e688-c42a-58a8-aa2e-6c419db98208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257942} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4aecba-9357-5bb7-978f-a673979c6b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258268} {:answer/id #uuid "b24f29ad-ae26-5c1a-98cc-8488a4660ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa4aecba-9357-5bb7-978f-a673979c6b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258268} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed6599f-2974-5160-b981-962e1c285d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2755d3ad-f502-5322-8d2b-3addfcafe3f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124750" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd8650a856fe3136759d81a886b2d4622cf3eb463b75e6023b44083c72f63a1a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/734eef45985ddfb6450666e1dd32c6d3beab9ca3aef66c87c6ba393d6960b08b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{2\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9081c344-3a39-5a06-bd28-8b285c2ea9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109772} {:answer/id #uuid "93524fd2-35f0-5d71-8d9e-e239e386e599" :answe